    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-28058",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{1}{6}(x-2)(x-3)(x+4)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that the function is a product of $3$ linear binomials, so this function is a cubic function. Moreover, it is a positive cubic because if we were to expand the parentheses, the coefficient of the ${x}^{3}$ term would be positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6}(x-2)(x-3)(x+4)=\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x+⋯=\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}+⋯\n$$\n\nThe equation is already in factored form. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the roots are $x=2,$ $x=3,$ and $x=-4.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{6}(0-2)(0-3)(0+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)(-3)(4)}{6} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an inverted $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-4,2,3$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=4,$ as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234519/assets/1f574e24288b0094defccb791407c74d9c3a5eeb1821b3bcac3bfa88153e69f5.png)",
